To focus on an element found by `getElementsByTagName` in JavaScript, you can use the `focus()` method on the resulting HTMLCollection. Here's an example:
```
const elements = document.getElementsByTagName("my-tag-name");
elements[0].focus(); // focus on the first element with the tag name "my-tag-name"
```
Note that `getElementsByTagName` returns an HTMLCollection, which is a collection of elements that match the specified tag name. To access a specific element in the collection, you need to specify an index, like `elements[0]`.

Also, keep in mind that the `focus()` method only works on elements that can receive focus, such as input elements, links, and some others. If the element you're trying to focus is not focusable, the `focus()` method will have no effect.

If you want to focus on a specific element that doesn't have a unique ID, you can loop through the collection and check if the element is the one you're looking for:
```
const elements = document.getElementsByTagName("my-tag-name");
for (let i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  const element = elements[i];
  // check if this is the element you want to focus
  if (element === yourTargetElement) {
    element.focus();
    break;
  }
}
```
Make sure to replace `yourTargetElement` with the actual element you want to focus.

