Here is a simple example of a fractal heart in p5.js and WebGL. This example uses the "Hilbert Curve" algorithm to generate the outline of the heart. The heart is drawn in a red color.

```javascript
let angle = 0;
let scale = 0.01;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(600, 600, WEBGL);
  stroke(255, 0, 0); // Red color
  strokeWeight(2);
}

function draw() {
  background(0);

  translate(0, 0, -200);
  rotateX(GRADIANS + angle);
  rotateY(GRADIANS + angle);

  drawHilbert(0, -200, 150);

  angle += 0.01;
}

function drawHilbert(x, y, depth) {
  if (depth > 1) {
    // Draw four smaller Hilbert curves
    strokeWeight(0.5);
    drawHilbert(x - depth * scale, y + depth * scale, depth - 1);
    rotateZ(GRADIANS);
    drawHilbert(x + depth * scale, y + depth * scale, depth - 1);
    rotateZ(-GRADIANS * 2);
    drawHilbert(x + depth * scale, y - depth * scale, depth - 1);
    rotateZ(GRADIANS);
    drawHilbert(x - depth * scale, y - depth * scale, depth - 1);
  } else {
    // Draw a line if the depth is 1
    line(x, y, x + depth * scale, y);
    line(x + depth * scale, y, x + depth * scale, y + depth * scale);
    line(x + depth * scale, y + depth * scale, x, y + depth * scale);
    line(x, y + depth * scale, x, y);
  }
}
```

This code creates a rotating 3D heart in the center of the canvas. The heart is made up of four Hilbert curves. The `drawHilbert` function recursively draws the Hilbert curves.

You can adjust the `scale` and `depth` variables in the `setup` function to control the size and complexity of the heart.

